  "text": "ארוח\\[ P = 1, \\ Q = 1, \\ R = 0, \\ S = 0, \\ T = 0.\\] etermine the locus of the centers $(a, b)$ of all circles that are tangent to both the origin and the $y$-axis. Identify a known geometric shape that fits this condition. With the assistance of a sketch, determine all the integer coordinate points on this shape. Documentation of this is similar to 6/2, volume 8, issue 1, chapter G, page 398, from the Cuban Mathematical Olympiad. The correct way to solve this problem involves careful consideration of the geometric constraints and the algebraic equations involved. Here is a step-by-step approach:\n\n### Step 1: Understand the problem\nWe need to find the locus of the centers of all circles that are tangent to both the origin \\((0,0)\\) and the \\(y\\)-axis \\((0,0)\\) and intersect the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) at two distinct points, such that the line segment connecting these points is perpendicular to the tangent line to the parabola at its midpoint.\n\n### Step 2: Geometry of the problem\nA circle tangent to both the origin \\((0,0)\\) and the \\(y\\)-axis must be of the form \\((a, b)^T + s(1, 0)^T\\), where \\(s, b > 0\\). This is because the center of the circle \\((a, b)\\) must lie along the line \\(s(1, 0)\\), which is perpendicular to the \\(y\\)-axis.\n\nThe tangent line to the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) that is relevant is when \\(s\\) is equal to the parameter \\(m\\) such that the tangent line at \\((1,2m)\\) is \\(y = \\frac{1}{2m}x + 2m^2\\). The slope of this line is \\(\\frac{1}{2m}\\) and is equal to the slope of the diameter perpendicular to the line of tangency, so\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2m} = -\\frac{b}{2a}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the relationship between \\(a, b,\\) and \\(m\\).\n\n### Step 3: Relate points of intersection\nLet the circle intersect the parabola at two points \\((a_1, b_1)\\) and \\((a_2, b_2)\\). The midpoint of this segment is \\((\\frac{a_1+a_2}{2}, \\frac{b_1+b_2}{2})\\). The tangents to the parabola at these points intersect on the \\(y\\)-axis at the coefficient of \\(s\\) in the equation of the circle that is tangent to the parabola, which is \\(b\\). The midpoint of the segment connecting these points is the \\(y\\)-intercept of the tangent line perpendicular to the line of tangency.\n\nThe midpoint of the segment from the tangent point to the parabola intersection point is \\(\\left(\\frac{a_1+a_2}{2}, \\frac{b_1+b_2}{2}\\right)\\). The equation of the tangent to the parabola at \\((a_1, b_1)\\) and \\((a_2, b_2)\\), is derived from the formula \\((\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1})\\). The slope of the tangent is \\(\\frac{b_1}{a_1}\\) and \\(\\frac{b_2}{a_2}\\), the midpoint of the segment perpendicularly below this is \\(\\left(\\frac{a_1+a_2}{2}, \\frac{b_1+b_2}{2}\\right)\\). The midpoint is the center of this circle.\n\nDistributing \\(a_1\\) and \\(a_2\\) to \\(b_1\\) and \\(b_2\\) and replacing, considering the conditions, we get:\n\\[\nb_1^2 = 4a_1A, b_2^2 = 4a_2A:\n\\]\n\nThis can be consolidated into the eccentric subtending relation \\(\\frac{a}{a_1},\\) which simplifies to one using the two circles relation defined. \\[\\frac{2a(b_1+a_1)}{b} = \\frac{2a(b_2+b_2)}{2b_2}\\).\n\nThis gives us the equation we were solving:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Locus} \\rightarrow 1a^2 + 1b^2 + 0a + 0b + 0 = 0.}\n\\]\n\nарוחית Removed dependencies for this:",
